ard/default configuration\n- Provides a balanced split where the CCS subsystem handles compute workloads normally\n- Recommended for general use and most compute tasks\n\n**CCS Mode 2**\n- An alternative partitioning scheme\n- Typically shifts more resources toward either compute or graphics depending on the hardware revision\n- Useful when you're hitting resource contention between compute and graphics operations\n\n**CCS Mode 4**\n- This is where I need to be honest about my uncertainty\n\n## My Honest Caveat\n\nI'm not fully confident in the precise technical distinctions between these specific modes for the `xe` driver, as this is fairly specialized hardware configuration detail that I may not have reliable information on. The exact semantics can also vary between:\n\n- Different firmware versions\n- Different Arc GPU revisions (the A770 has multiple vendors/PCB revisions)\n- Kernel and driver version combinations\n\n## What I'd Recommend\n\nSince getting this wrong could affect your workload performance, I'd suggest verifying against:\n\n1. **Your kernel's documentation** \u2014 check `man` pages or driver docs installed with your kernel\n2. **Intel's Arc Linux driver documentation** at intel.com\n3. **The `xe` driver source** or Intel's oneAPI/Compute docs, which often explain these modes in detail\n\nWould you be able to share more about your specific use case? That would help me give more targeted guidance on which mode might suit your needs."}